565 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 301 0 R
/T (Improved DCGAN)
/E (Improved DCGAN)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
566 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 301 0 R
/E (Option 1 showed that the baseline DCGAN's main problem was discriminator dominance, it learned to separate real from fake too easily, leaving the generator with vanishing gradients. In this model we apply three standard techniques to address this:)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
567 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1170 0 R
/E (Spectral Normalisation on the discriminator: constrains the Lipschitz constant of each layer, preventing the discriminator from becoming arbitrarily confident. This replaces BatchNorm in the discriminator.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
568 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1171 0 R
/E (Label Smoothing \(0.15\): instead of training the discriminator with hard labels \(real=1, fake=0\), we use real=0.85. This softens the discriminator's confidence and keeps the gradient signal alive.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
569 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1172 0 R
/E (Two Time-Scale Update Rule \(TTUR\): the generator uses a higher learning rate \(2e-4\) than the discriminator \(1e-4\), giving the generator a head start in each training step.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
570 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1173 0 R
/E ( Epochs Runned: 100.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
571 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 301 0 R
/E (We also increase the generator capacity from ngf=64 to ngf=128, doubling the number of filters per layer so it can model finer facial details.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
572 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /Figure
/P 301 0 R
/Pg 37 0 R
/A [1174 0 R 0]
/K [8]
>>
endobj
