/P 302 0 R
/T (MODEL ARCHITECTURE & DESIGN CHOICES)
/E (MODEL ARCHITECTURE & DESIGN CHOICES)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
574 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 302 0 R
/T (WGAN-GP \(Wasserstein GAN with Gradient Penalty\))
/E (WGAN-GP \(Wasserstein GAN with Gradient Penalty\))
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
575 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 302 0 R
/E (Options 1 and 2 showed that BCE loss is fundamentally problematic for GAN training, it either saturates when the discriminator is too strong \(Option 1\) or produces a lazy equilibrium when the discriminator is constrained \(Option 2\).)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
576 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 302 0 R
/E (This model replaces BCE with the Wasserstein distance. Provides smooth, non-saturating gradients regardless of discriminator strength.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
577 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 302 0 R
/E (The key changes from the Improved DCGAN \(Option 2\):)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
578 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1175 0 R
/E (Wasserstein loss: the critic maximises E[C\(real\)] - E[C\(fake\)], the generator minimises -E[C\(fake\)]. No log, no saturation.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
579 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1176 0 R
/E (Gradient Penalty \(GP\): replaces weight clipping. Enforces a 1-Lipschitz constraint by penalising the critic when its gradient norm deviates from 1. This is computed on interpolations between real and fake images.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
580 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1177 0 R
/E (InstanceNorm instead of BatchNorm in the critic: BatchNorm is incompatible with gradient penalty because GP requires per-sample gradients, while BatchNorm couples samples within a batch.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
581 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1178 0 R
/E (N_critic = 2: the critic is trained 2 steps for every 1 generator step, giving it a more accurate distance estimate.)
/Pg 38 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
